    <% \} else \{ %>\par
        <a href="/login" class="auth-btn">Login</a>\par
        <a href="/register" class="auth-btn">Register</a>\par
    <% \} %>\par
</nav>\par
\par
[Coach]: This is a very clean, structured EJS navigation file, Sampson. Safely checking locals.session and locals.isLoggedIn prevents runtime reference errors if those variables aren't passed to a view. Looking at your conditional logic, you check session.user.role === 'admin' to render administrative links in the browser UI. Since you are already using server-side middleware (like requireRole) to protect the actual endpoints on the backend, why is it still necessary to explicitly write this extra conditional code to hide the links in the EJS frontend view?\par
\par
[Sampson]: Hiding links on the frontend is necessary for two simple reasons:\par
1. Better User Experience: It prevents confusion. If users see an administrative link and click it, only to be blocked by an error screen, it looks like the website is broken. Hiding the link keeps the interface clean and relevant to what they can actually do.\par
2. Basic Security (Hiding the Target): It hides the layout of the system. If standard users can see the links, they instantly know the exact web addresses of your private admin pages (like /users). Hiding the links forces bad actors to guess the paths, reducing the chances of someone trying to attack or exploit those specific pages. The backend middleware is the actual lock on the door, while the frontend code hides the door from plain sight.\par
\par
[Coach]: Exactly right, Sampson! The frontend logic provides a clean interface and prevents unnecessary friction, while the backend middleware handles the actual enforcement. "Hiding the door versus locking it" is the perfect analogy for defense-in-depth. We have successfully covered all four required topics. You did an absolute standout job walking through the security, performance, and architecture trade-offs today. Your deep understanding of these concepts is very clear.\par
